  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Parish Noticeboard",
    "overview": "SPAOC reads like the noticeboard in the church hall: what is on this week, laid out so anyone can find it in a glance. Content sits on plain white cards over a soft grey ground, each event tagged by a coloured bar that carries its calendar's identity. Navy and bronze do the chrome work; everything else is neutral, so the calendar colours are the loudest thing on screen.\n\nThe system is plain and sturdy. Type is the platform's own, sized generously for older eyes; cards are rounded and lightly lifted; targets are large; navigation is the OS's native tab bar. There is little ornament. The one decorative motif is the eight-pointed star illustration, tinted in the primary colour, which closes the About screen and marks the parish's own voice. Per-calendar Orthodox icon images appear on event detail screens.\n\nLight and dark are both first-class. Dark mode swaps navy for bronze as the lead accent, since navy loses contrast on near-black.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "White cards on a soft grey ground in light mode; `#272727` cards on `#121212` in dark.",
      "Calendar colour is expressed as a vertical bar, never as a card fill.",
      "One lead accent per scheme (navy light, bronze dark); bronze is the secondary in light.",
      "System font only, with 16px as the floor for reading text.",
      "Native tab bar and platform haptics; no custom shell."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Calendar Colour Rule",
        "body": "A calendar's colour identifies its events and nothing else. It appears as the 8px bar on a row, never as a button, a text colour or a card fill.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Lead Accent Rule",
        "body": "Each scheme has one lead accent for interactive chrome: navy in light, bronze in dark. Do not use both as competing actions on the same screen.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Red Rule",
        "body": "Donate Red is reserved for giving. Errors and destructive actions do not borrow it.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The 16px Floor Rule",
        "body": "Content a parishioner has to read is 16px or larger. 14px is for a time or a caption beside a larger title.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Italic Aside Rule",
        "body": "Italics are reserved for the subtitle under a screen title and for scripture quotes (the Donate verse); they are never used for emphasis in lists.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Soft Lift Rule",
        "body": "Shadow opacity stays at or below 0.2 and radius at or below 4. A card is lifted, never floating.",
